ell 9% y/y, the steepest drop among large makers (n3.d2).\n- Voltaris's share of Caldrian shipments fell from 31% to 27% in Q2 2026. Tarnwell and imports gained (n3.d3).\n- Voltaris quarterly revenue went C$905M, 960M, 980M, 890M from 2025Q3 to 2026Q2. That is a C$90M (9%) sequential drop in Q2 (n2.d7).\n- The Q2 2026 run-rate is C$3.56B a year (4 × 890M).\n- The 2025Q3–2026Q2 total is C$3.735B.\n- Shipments typically trail orders by about two quarters. The Q2 2026 weakness therefore reflects orders from around Q4 2025. Weakness in the 2026H2 and 2027 shipments data would come from orders placed in 2026, which I don't have.\n\n**Implications for 2027**\n- C$4.2B needs about C$1.05B per quarter. That is about 18% above the Q2 level and about 7% above the best recent quarter (980M).\n- The company would need to regain its share and see industry growth. Share is moving the wrong way, from 31% to 27%.\n- If share stays at 27% and the industry is flat, revenue stays near C$3.5–3.6B.\n- If industry volumes recover 3–5% a year, the 2027 estimate is about C$3.7–3.9B.\n- Regaining 31% share would add roughly 15% (31/27). That would make C$4.1–4.4B possible, but the data shows no sign of it.\n- Price increases and mix could add some revenue. I have no data on them.\n\n**Caveats**\n- This rests on one quarter of data, and revenue and shipments are not the same thing.\n- Backlog and order data (n2's judgment) could change this. If it shows a strong order book, the shipment weakness could be temporary.","ttft_ms":4310,"type":"result","duration_ms":21664,"uuid":"c26f09d7-669d-43a4-9c1b-81116e968a3a","ttft_stream_ms":3498,"time_to_request_ms":276,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":29,"system_prompt":27,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":17,"input_attachments":48,"input_hooks":11,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":15,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":27,"init_frame_send":9,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":14,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"re